      "sample_index": 4,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to understand that similar triangles have corresponding sides that are proportional. We start by identifying the sides of the original isosceles triangle and the longest side of the similar triangle.\n\nThe original isosceles triangle has sides of lengths 8 cm, 8 cm, and 10 cm. The longest side of this triangle is 10 cm. The similar triangle has a longest side of 25 cm.\n\nSince the triangles are similar, the ratio of the longest sides of the two triangles is the same as the ratio of their corresponding sides. We can set up a proportion to find the scaling factor:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{25}{10} = \\frac{25}{8}\n\\]\n\nThis tells us that each side of the larger triangle is \\(\\frac{25}{8}\\) times the corresponding side of the smaller triangle. Therefore, the sides of the larger triangle are:\n\n\\[\n8 \\times \\frac{25}{8} = 25 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\\[\n8 \\times \\frac{25}{8} = 25 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\\[\n10 \\times \\frac{25}{8} = \\frac{250}{8} = 31.25 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nNow, we can find the perimeter of the larger triangle by summing the lengths of its sides:\n\n\\[\n25 + 25 + 31.25 = 81.25 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nThus, the perimeter of the larger triangle is \\(\\boxed{81.25}\\) cm.",
